Based on your interests in screenwriting, storytelling, public speaking, organizing, and potential business aspirations, here are three highly-regarded universities in the UK and EU, each with a specific Bachelor's program that aligns well with your profile:

    University of Birmingham (UK)[1][2]

+ Program: BA Film and Creative Writing[1]

        Why it fits: This program directly combines your interest in film/screenwriting with creative writing. It emphasizes practical skills in both filmmaking and writing (including screenplays), guided by industry experts.[1][3] The course explores film, television, and writing in critical and industry contexts, which could align with your interest in the business side. The University of Birmingham is highly ranked for Creative Writing in the UK.[1]

    Kingston University London (UK)

+ Program: BA (Hons) Creative Writing[4][5][6]

        Why it fits: While a broader Creative Writing degree, Kingston's program explicitly includes learning the "art and craft of writing in all its forms, including... screenwriting". It offers workshops to hone craft, masterclasses by industry professionals, and opportunities to write for various media (including radio and screen).[5] Being part of Kingston School of Art provides a collaborative and creative community environment.[5] This focus on diverse writing forms supports storytelling and communication skills relevant to screenwriting and presenting.[7]

    Universidad Carlos III de Madrid (UC3M) (Spain - EU)

+ Program: Bachelor in Film, Television and Media Studies (Bilingual - Spanish/English)[8]

        Why it fits: This EU option offers a comprehensive understanding of audiovisual media in the digital age. It focuses on training professionals in audiovisual production, distribution, television, radio, and online content management, touching upon your interests in organization and potential business aspects. Crucially, it includes training in technologies applied to scripts (screenwriting), production, editing, and multimedia communication.[8] The program is taught bilingually (English and Spanish) and includes practical work in university studios.[8] Note: Requires B2 level English proficiency.[8]